<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  The action buttons lie in the free ground of the table that config/TableLayout.xml gives each shape (Table/TableRegions.h): the
  stack ActionRow fills the region table.actions, the label Hint the region table.hint. The regions are clear of every tile and plate
  (TableLayoutSpecTest, MahjongTableScreenTest) and win over the safe area: the table is a fixed design. The buttons start hidden; the
  component shows the ones the person to act can press. They wrap inside the stack, so the region's width decides the lines. A button
  says what it does in a word (Table/ActionButtons.h: the keys button.<Kind>) and, where it concerns tiles, shows them as UIImage
  children NameTile0..2 that take no input, so a click on a picture is a click on the button. The label Hint shows the sentence of the
  button under the pointer ({actionHint}); it takes no input.
  Each button has the skin of its role (config/CLAUDE.md, "UI style": gradient to the <role>Deep, border, shadow, bold text); ActionsCompact the same.
  The variants are sizes of the buttons (a size class, not a place): portrait and narrow tables. Which sizes: kActionButtonHeight and
  kPortraitActionButtonHeight (Table/TouchRegime.h), TouchRegimeTest and MahjongTableScreenTest.

  Two canvases of the same buttons, Actions (this) and ActionsCompact, and the component shows one (NeedsCompactActions, which counts the
  bottom inset). Why two: a SceneView canvas is scaled by the table's fit, has no minimum scale, and a variant sees view units, never
  output pixels, so no variant of this canvas can make a button bigger in a small window. ActionsCompact is a Screen canvas with
  minScale and serves the windows where these buttons are under 32 px and the bar under the table holds its row above the safe area.
  Both hold the same 13 elements, equal by name, action, value, word and picture (MahjongTableScreenTest compares them).
  Named deviations, where this canvas stays under 32 px: the 1:1 portrait window of 480 px (25.5 px; the table fills the window, so
  there is no bar for a bigger row) and the near-square narrow window with side insets (610 x 600: 31.6 px, clear of the hand, because
  the row of ActionsCompact would lie on the tiles). MahjongTouchSizeTest names them in ActionsMayBeSmall.
-->
